---
/**
 * Section 01 — the fork-vs-restart discriminator, which is the whole product.
 *
 * The comparison is against restart files, not against rerunning from t = 0.
 * Every production code restarts from a checkpoint — Fluent case-plus-data, an
 * SU2 restart_flow.dat, an OpenFOAM time directory — so a page that competes
 * against rerunning from initial conditions is arguing with nobody.
 *
 * The coupled-physics claim rests on the retropulsion roster, not the corridor.
 * Across the corridor's bank branches the flow observables agree to three
 * digits (its own gate 4a says so); only the trajectory moves. Retropulsion is
 * the shipped case where the branches genuinely change the flow.
 *
 * The fork tree diagram lands in the next phase. A placeholder would be worse
 * than its absence.
 */
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import ForkTree from './ForkTree.astro';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };

/* corridor/main.rs:84-90 and :98-115, condensed. `.trigger` is kept: it is the
   mechanism the lede's "predicate on the flow" claim rests on. */
const flow = `// Trajectory: march until the evolved sheath's n_e crosses the GPS L1 cutoff.
let onset = CfdFlow::march(&nominal)
    .couple(world::corridor_coupling(1.0, 0))
    .trigger(trigger)
    .from_field(world::initial_field())
    .until(|field, _| field.regime().map(|r| r.gnss_denied).unwrap_or(false))?;

// Campaign: fork that one pause per candidate command, fly every branch
// concurrently, score the rows, then refine around the winner from the
// same onset and gate the two-round result.
let corridor = CfdFlow::study("bank-angle corridor")
    .cases(model::coarse_commands())
    .fork(&onset)                            // the shared flow-resolved fork point
    .branch(model::bank_world)               // one alternated world per command
    .continue_for(constants::BRANCH_STEPS)   // concurrent, copy-on-write
    .reduce_all(model::score_branches)
    .refine(&onset, model::fine_candidates)  // second round, same paused onset
    .branch(model::bank_world)
    .continue_for(constants::BRANCH_STEPS)
    .reduce_all(model::score_branches)
    .gates(model::corridor_gates())
    .verdict()?;`;

/* retropulsion/output.txt:24-29, verbatim. */
const roster = `  branch         cmd    flown  preserved  axial m/s2    prop kg     dv m/s  dv frozen
  coast         0.00   0.0000          —     10.5950       1.81    139.519      0.144
  low           0.20   0.2000     0.2510      7.4718      62.05     88.399     49.490
  mid           0.40   0.4000     0.1238      9.9667     122.30    120.335     99.984
  high          0.60   0.6000    -0.0161     12.8932     182.54    151.991    151.413
  hard          0.85   0.7931    -0.0611     17.1977     256.80    212.085    216.209`;
---
<section class="cfd-sec page" data-rise>
  <header class="sec-head">
    <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-coord">
      <span class="ix">01 / 05</span><span class="lb">Forking a solved case</span>
    </p>
    <h2>Branch from the state the vehicle reached</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      The fork point is a predicate on the flow, not a step number. The corridor
      example, a reentry descent through comms blackout, marches until the
      evolved sheath's electron density crosses the GPS L1 cutoff, then hands
      back a state every branch continues from.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="panel corner-brackets body-panel rise">
    <p class="body">
      You already restart from checkpoints: a Fluent case and data pair, an SU2
      <code>restart_flow.dat</code>, an OpenFOAM time directory, a shell loop
      around them. A fork differs in four ways. The pause is chosen by a
      predicate on the evolved state, not a time picked in advance. Every branch
      stamps a marker naming the baseline it replaced. The branches run
      concurrently in one process, sharing the marched state by reference. And
      the set of branches, which the run calls a roster, reduces to one table of
      scored rows with a pass or fail on each.
    </p>

    <p class="body">
      The figure below traces that shape left to right. A single march runs to
      the pause, five branches leave that one point and run side by side, and
      the collector on the right is the verdict they reduce to. The accent line
      through the middle is the branch the run committed.
    </p>

    <ForkTree />

    <Code code={flow} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />

    <h3>What the coupling is worth</h3>

    <p class="body">
      A second example, a powered landing under retropropulsion, forks a
      mid-burn state five ways at different throttle settings. For every branch
      it also computes what thrust-only kinematics would have predicted, with
      the flow closure frozen at the fork. Its roster prints the coupled answer
      and that decoupled prediction side by side, in the last two columns:
    </p>

    <Code code={roster} lang="text" themes={SHIKI} />

    <p class="body">
      Read down the <code>axial m/s2</code> column, which is the deceleration
      each branch actually flew. It is non-monotone in throttle: the 20% branch
      sheds less speed than the coasting one. The <code>preserved</code> column
      explains why, falling through zero as the throttles harden, because the
      plume reorganises the drag the vehicle sees. A campaign that solved the
      drag model separately and composed afterwards would get the
      <code>dv frozen</code> column, which misses that ordering by 38.9 m/s on
      the branch where it matters most.
    </p>

    <p class="body">
      Not every fork earns that. The corridor's bank branches agree to three
      digits in the flow, so only their trajectories differ, and the corridor is
      a weaker demonstration of coupling for exactly that reason. The
      retropulsion roster is where branches change the flow, gated at a spread
      of 0.0202 against a 0.0150 threshold.
    </p>

    <p class="more">
      <a href="/tutorial/stage-1-corridor/">Walk 1: how the fork is built</a>
      <a href="/blueprints/fork-a-running-simulation/">Blueprint: fork a running simulation</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

